    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-5307",
      "problem": "Sketch the graph of $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x+2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14242844/assets/ffd98b8f9b6c7a9260f43772f9c654901bd02c21efa1eb06f2a5fdad8b263729.png)\n\nSince we have $y = \\dfrac{1}{x \\color{red} + 2},$ we need to translate the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ to the *left* by $\\color{red}2$ units.\n\nNow, since the graph of $y = \\dfrac 1 x$ is translated to the left by $2$ units, the vertical asymptote $x = 0$ is also translated to the left by $2$ units. So, the equation of the new vertical asymptote is $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14242844/assets/e8bff71235e67d8ad4046e94b5fe2682543674877a253ae6d4fe431504dbde3c.png)",
